          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does COPD guidance describe about when to add an inhaled corticosteroid?",
          "answer": "The COPD guidance (*Global respiratory strategy report, educational summary*) describes inhaled corticosteroids as an add-on for a specific group. Its passages say:\n\n- **Who it is considered for:** People with a significant history of flare-ups (exacerbations) who are already on two long-acting bronchodilators (a muscarinic antagonist and a beta-agonist). An inhaled corticosteroid is added where the **blood eosinophil count** is high, or where features of coexisting asthma are present.\n- **Why a marker is used:** The blood eosinophil count predicts who benefits. The guidance avoids giving it to everyone because inhaled corticosteroids in this group carry an increased risk of pneumonia. The benefit needs to be identifiable to be worth that risk.\n\nThe passage I received is a partial extract, so it may not list every criterion. It also does not give thresholds for the eosinophil count, and it does not cover doses. Those details would need a clinician or the full guideline.\n\nThe asthma guidance in the results is a different condition, so I have not used it here.\n\nIf you are asking about your own situation, a respiratory clinician can check your flare-up history, blood test results and inhaler use and explain how this guidance applies to you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
